  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1218 AM CDT Thu Oct 8 2026\n\nToday - Sunday: Mostly Dry with Warm Temperatures, Breezy on Saturday\n\nNorthwesterly flow will prevail through Friday before flow begins to \nturn zonal into the weekend as a more pronounced upper-level ridge \npushes into the Upper Midwest. Consequently, temperature trends will \nstay slightly above normal with highs generally in the upper 60s to \nmiddle 70s. However, as we head into the daytime hours on Friday, \n850mb warm air advection will push through the region, allowing for \nsome precipitation chances south of I-90. This is shown in the grand \nensemble (GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) which has modest probabilities \n(50-80%) for measurable rainfall for areas south of I-90. However, \ngiven the ample dry air this warm air advection would be running \ninto and the rapid progressing nature of this forcing, generally \nexpecting any rainfall totals to remain under 0.1\". \n\nAs we head into the weekend, the aforementioned upper-level ridging \nbegins to move overhead with southerly surface flow resulting from \nthe aforementioned warm air advection. Consequently, expecting \ntemperatures to warm up noticeably into the weekend with the inter-\nquartile range for highs in the NBM ranging from the upper 70s to \nmiddle 80s, roughly 15-20 degrees above normal for this time of \nyear. In addition to the warmer temperatures, both an increasing \nsurface pressure gradient and favorable diurnal mixing into a 35-40 \nkt low-level jet will enable momentum transfer of stronger wind \ngusts to the surface on Saturday afternoon. As a result, the NBM has \nfairly high probabilities (40-70%) for wind gusts over 40 mph, \nmainly across southeast MN. There is even some potential (10-40%) \nfor wind gusts over 45 mph as shown in the NBM across southeast MN \nas well. Otherwise, winds generally diminish on Sunday with slightly \ncooler temperatures as the aforementioned upper-level ridge moves \noff to the east. \n\nEarly Next Week: Cooler with Rain Chances \n\nBy the time next work week rolls around, a notable change in \nconditions occurs as the aforementioned upper-level ridge pulls east \nand is replaced by an approaching shortwave trough descending into \nthe Upper Midwest from south-central Canada. As this occurs, \ndeterministic guidance generally agrees on pushing a surface low \nnorth of the region with an attending surface cold front progressing \nthrough the area on Monday. Consequently, expecting some rain \nchances throughout much of the day on Monday with the grand ensemble \n(GEFS/EC/Canadian ensemble) having decent probabilities (60-80%) for \nmeasurable rainfall across the region. They key question that \nremains though is exactly how amplified the previously stated \nshortwave will be and the exact timing of its passage. How these \ntrends ultimately pan out will dictate exact location and timing for \nany precipitation. In any case, temperatures will likely note a \nsubstantial cooling trend by Tuesday with the inter-quartile range \nof the NBM having high temperatures near to slightly below normal \nand ranging from the lower 50s to middle 60s.",
